nodecode-federated-search is a rewrite of kumaxs's kumaxs/hermes-federated-search:

  https://github.com/kumaxs/hermes-federated-search
  commit 566b3ccc6aeff7e39c19dcfd779ade107827043c
  Apache-2.0, Copyright (c) 2026 kumaxs (LICENSE; its appendix placeholder is unfilled)

The original was a Hermes web-search provider named `federated', selected with
`web.search_backend: federated', plus a settings card in the Hermes desktop app.
Its provider read a `backends' list out of the config and asked each line -- a
registered provider, or any HTTP endpoint taking the query as a parameter -- in
parallel under one deadline, merged what came back, deduplicated it by url,
ranked it by keyword score (optionally by an auxiliary LLM), and answered the
top of it, honouring `required' lines and `min_backends'. A line could carry
several keys, listed by environment variable name, and rotate or fail over
between them with a cooldown. All of that semantic is kept here: the same
`backends' array of line objects, the same `timeout', `max_results',
`min_backends' and `pool_strategy' keys, the same three response shapes
(organic / data.web / results), the same keyword ranking, the same dedupe and
the same failover and cooldown times. What changed is the surface: Nodecode's
websearch cell keeps its engines in a fixed list a cell cannot add one to, so
the fan-out is this cell's own tool, `federated_search', and `/federated-search'
is the operator's way in. What was left out: the LLM re-ranking (a cell would
have to drive a model of its own, and keyword ranking is the original's own
default and its fallback), the health-check HEAD probes (a line that is down
now costs its own timeout, as it did with the probes off), the Hermes desktop
card editor, and the `k' alias for the line count, which the array's own
length now says. Nothing of the original is vendored.
